\v 77 Merari's¦190562 descendants¦190561 \add received these cities and\add* their nearby pasturelands from¦190564 the¦190564 Zevulun¦190565 tribe¦190564: Rimon¦190568 and¦190569 Tavor¦190574.
\p
\v 78 From¦190580 the¦190580 Re'uven¦190586 tribe¦190585 across the¦190581 Yarden¦190581 \add !(Jordan¦190581)\add* \add River\add* (opposite Yeriho¦190582 \add !(Jericho¦190582)\add*), \add they were allotted these cities and towns\add* and their nearby pastureland: Betser¦190589 in¦190590 the wilderness¦190590, Yahats¦190596,
\v 79 Kedemot¦190604, and Mofa'at¦190610.
\p
\v 80 From¦190616 the¦190616 Gad¦190618 tribe¦190616, \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Ramot¦190621 in¦190622 the Gilead¦190622 \add region\add*, Mahanayim¦190628,
\v 81 Heshbon¦190636, and Yazer¦190642.
\c 7
\s1 The descendants of Yisakar (Issachar)
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Issachar; Issachar; Descendants of Issachar
\p
\v 1 Yisakar's¦190649 \add !(Issachar¦190649)\add* four¦190655 sons¦190648 were Tola¦190650, Puah¦190651, Yashuv¦190652, and¦190654 Shimron¦190654.
\v 2 Tola's¦190659 sons¦190658 were¦190660 Uzi¦190660, Refayah¦190661, Yeriel¦190662, Yahmay¦190663, Yivsam¦190664, and¦190665 Shmuel¦190665 \add !(Samuel¦190665)\add*, \add who all became\add* leaders of their clans. \add ≈There were strong warriors in the record of Tola's¦190670 descendants in each generation¦190673\add*. (During¦190675 the¦190675 time¦190675 when David¦190676 was¦190677 Yisrael's \add !(Israel)\add* king, there were 22,600 such men.)
\p
\v 3 Uzi's¦190686 son was¦190687 Yizrahyah¦190687. Yizrahyah's¦190689 five¦190694 sons¦190688 were¦190689 Mika'el¦190690 \add !(Michael¦190690)\add*, Ovadyah¦190691 \add !(Obadiah¦190691)\add*, Yoel¦190692 \add !(Joel¦190692)\add*, and Yishiyah¦190693. They were¦190695 all¦190696 \add ≈leaders¦190695 of¦190696 their clans\add*.
\v 4 Along with¦190698 them¦190698, according to¦190699 their¦190699 \add ≈family¦190700 records\add*, were 36,000 troops¦190702 in the army, because¦190708 they¦190710 \add ≈had¦190710 numerous\add* wives¦190711 and¦190712 sons¦190712.
\v 5 Altogether, Yisakar's¦190717 clans¦190716 had 87,000 \add ≈strong\add* warriors listed in their¦190723 genealogy¦190723.
\s1 Benyamin's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Benjamin; Benjamin; The Descendants of Benjamin and Dan
\p
\v 6 Benyamin's¦190727 three¦190731 \add ≡sons\add* were¦190728 Bela¦190728, Beker¦190729, and¦190730 Yedieael¦190730.
\v 7 Bela's¦190734 five¦190740 sons¦190733 were¦190735 Etsbon¦190735, Uzi¦190736, Uziel¦190737, Yerimot¦190738, and¦190739 Iri¦190739, who were leaders of¦190741 their clans. According to their family¦190742 records¦190746, they had 22,034 \add ≈strong\add* warriors.
\p
\v 8 Beker's¦190755 sons were¦190756 Zemirah¦190756, Yoash¦190757, Eliezer¦190758, Elyehoeynay¦190759, Omri¦190760, Yerimot¦190761, Aviyah¦190762, Anatot¦190763, and¦190764 Alemet¦190764.
\v 9 Altogether, their clans had 20,200 \add ≈strong\add* warriors listed in their genealogy.
\p
\v 10 Yedieael's¦190785 son was¦190786 Bilhan¦190786. Bilhan's¦190788 sons were Ye'ush¦190789, Benyamin¦190791, Ehud¦190792, Kena'anah¦190793, Zeytan¦190794, Tarshish¦190795, and¦190796 Ahishahar¦190796.
\v 11 All¦190798 those¦190800 descendants¦190801 of¦190801 Yedieael¦190802 were \add ≈clan leaders\add* and¦190811 \add ≈strong\add* warriors, having 17,200 men ready to go¦190812 to war¦190813.
\p
\v 12 The Shupim¦190816 and¦190817 Hupim¦190817 were¦190818 descendants¦190818 of¦190818 Ir¦190819, \add and\add* the Hushim¦190820 were¦190821 descendants¦190821 of¦190821 Aher¦190822.
\s1 The descendants of Naftali (Naphtali)
\rem /s1 Descendants of Naphtali; The Descendants of Naphtali; Naphtali
\p
\v 13 Naftali's¦190825 \add !(Naphtali¦190825)\add* sons were¦190826 Yahtsiel¦190826, Guni¦190827, Yetser¦190828, and¦190829 Shalum¦190829. (\add They were\add* descendants¦190830 of¦190830 \add Ya'akov's\add* \add !(Jacob)\add* \add slave-wife\add* Bilhah¦190831.)
\s1 Menasheh's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Manasseh; Manasseh; Descendants of Manasseh
\p
\v 14 Menasheh¦190835 \add !(Manasseh¦190835)\add* had sons¦190834 by his¦190839 \add ≈Arami\add* \add !(Syrian)\add* (Heb. Aramean¦190840) slave-wife. She¦190841 gave birth¦190841 to Asriel¦190836 and Makir¦190844 (\add ≈who fathered¦190845\add* Gilead¦190846).
\v 15 Makir¦190848 \add ≈married\add* a¦190850 wife¦190850 from the Hupim¦190851 and¦190852 Shupim¦190852. (His¦190854 sister's¦190854 name was¦190855 Ma'akah¦190855.) \add ≈Another\add* descendant was¦190858 Tselofehad¦190858, and¦190859 he had daughters¦190861.
\v 16 Makir's¦190867 wife¦190865 (\add also called\add* Ma'akah¦190864) bore¦190863 a¦190868 son¦190868 and¦190869 named¦190869 him Peresh¦190871 (and¦190872 named¦190872 his¦190873 brother¦190873 Sheresh¦190874) and¦190875 his¦190875 sons¦190875 were¦190876 Ulam¦190876 and¦190877 Rekem¦190877.
\v 17 Ulam's¦190880 son was¦190881 Bedan¦190881. Those¦190882 were¦190883 the¦190883 descendants¦190883 of¦190883 Gilead¦190884 (Makir's¦190887 son¦190888 and Menasheh's¦190890 grandson).
\p
\v 18 \add @Gilead's\add* sister¦190892 was Hamoleket¦190893. She¦190894 was the mother of Ishehod¦190897, Aviezer¦190900, and¦190901 Mahlah¦190903.
\p
\v 19 \add Another of Gilead's sons was\add* Shemida¦190907, whose sons¦190906 were¦190905 Ahyan¦190908, Shekem¦190909, Likhi¦190910, and¦190911 Anieam¦190911.
\s1 Efrayim's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Ephraim; The Descendants of Ephraim; Ephraim
\p
\v 20 These are Efrayim's \add !(Ephraim)\add* descendants¦190914:
\p One of¦190915 Efrayim's¦190915 sons¦190914 was¦190916 Shutelah¦190916. \add @Shutelah's\add* son was Bered¦190917. \add @Bered's¦190918\add* son¦190918 was Tahat¦190919. \add @Tahat's¦190920\add* son¦190920 was Eleadah¦190921. \add @Eleadah's\add* son \add was also named\add* Tahat¦190923.
\v 21 \add @Tahat's\add* son was Zavad¦190926. \add @Zavad's¦190927\add* son¦190927 was Shutelah¦190928. Efrayim's other sons¦190929, Ezer¦190930 and¦190931 Elead¦190931, went¦190939 to Gat¦190935 \add city\add* to¦190940 steal some cattle¦190943 but¦190932 were killed¦190932 by \add ≈some of¦190933 the¦190937 natives of¦190943 that area\add*.
\v 22 Their¦190947 father¦190947 Efrayim¦190946 mourned¦190945 for \add ≈quite some time¦190948\add*, and his relatives¦190951 came¦190950 to¦190952 comfort¦190952 him¦190952.
\v 23 Then¦190954 he¦190954 \add ≈slept with\add* his¦190957 wife¦190957 and¦190958 she¦190958 \add ≈got¦190958 pregnant¦190958\add* and¦190959 \add ≈gave¦190959 birth¦190959 to\add* a¦190960 son¦190960. \add @Efrayim\add* \add ≈named¦190964 him\add* Beriah¦190965 \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘trouble’)\add*, because¦190966 \add ≈of the recent tragedy in their family¦190969\add*.
\p
\v 24 \add @Efrayim's\add* daughter¦190971 was¦190972 She'erah¦190972 and¦190973 she¦190973 founded \add three towns\add*: Lower¦190979 Beyt¦190976-Horon¦190978, Upper¦190982 Beyt-Horon, and¦190983 Uzen¦190984-She'erah¦190985.
\p
\v 25 \add Another of Efrayim's sons\add* was Refah. Refah's son¦190988 was Reshef¦190989. \add @Reshef's\add* son was Telah¦190990. \add @Telah's¦190991\add* son¦190991 was Tahan¦190992.
\v 26 \add @Tahan's\add* son was Laedan¦190995. \add @Laedan's¦190996\add* son¦190996 was Amihud¦190997. \add @Amihud's¦190998\add* son¦190998 was Elishama¦190999.
\v 27 \add @Elishama's\add* son was Nun¦191002. \add @Nun's¦191003\add* son¦191003 was Yehoshua¦191004 \add !(Joshua¦191004)\add* \add (or Yehoshua, who led Yisrael's\add* \add !(Israel)\add* \add descendants after Mosheh\add* \add !(Moses)\add* \add died)\add*.
\p
\v 28 \add This is a list of\add* \add ≈the cities and areas where Efrayim's descendants lived¦191008\add*: Beyt¦191009-El¦191011 \add !(Bethel¦191009)\add* and¦191012 the nearby villages, Na'aran¦191014 to¦191013 the east¦191013, Gezer¦191016 to¦191015 the west¦191015 and¦191017 the nearby villages, and¦191018 Shekem¦191018 and¦191019 the nearby villages extending \add north\add* as far as Ayah¦191022 and¦191023 its¦191023 villages.
\p
\v 29 Along the¦191027 border of¦191027 the¦191028 area where Menasheh's¦191030 \add !(Manasseh¦191030)\add* descendants lived were these¦191041 towns: Beyt¦191031-Shean¦191033, Ta'anak¦191035, Megidon¦191037, Dor¦191039, and¦191040 all the nearby villages. The people who lived in¦191041 all those¦191041 places were descendants¦191043 of¦191043 Ya'akov's \add !(Jacob)\add* son¦191045 Yosef¦191044 \add !(Joseph¦191044)\add*.
\s1 Asher's descendants
\rem /s1 Asher; The Descendants of Asher
\p
\v 30 Asher's¦191051 sons¦191050 were¦191052 Yimnah¦191052, Yishvah¦191053, Yishvi¦191054, and¦191055 Beriah¦191055. Their¦191057 sister¦191057 was¦191057 Serah¦191056.
\p
\v 31 Beriah's¦191060 sons¦191059 were¦191061 Hever¦191061 and¦191062 Malkiel¦191062. \add @Malkiel¦191063\add* was¦191064 the¦191064 father¦191064 of¦191064 Birzot¦191065.
\p
\v 32 Hever¦191068 was the father¦191069 of Yaflet¦191072, Shomer¦191075, Hotam¦191078, and¦191079 their¦191081 sister¦191081 Shua¦191080.
\v 33 Yaflet's¦191084 sons were¦191085 Pasak¦191085, Bimhal¦191086, and¦191087 Ashvat¦191087.
\p
\v 34 Shomer's sons¦191092 were¦191094 Ahi¦191094, Rohagah¦191095, Yehubah¦191097, and¦191099 Aram¦191099.
\p
\v 35 \add Shomer's younger\add* brother¦191104 was Helem. Helem's sons¦191101 were¦191105 Tsofah¦191105, Yimna¦191106, Shelesh¦191107, and¦191108 Amal¦191108.
\p
\v 36 Tsofah's¦191111 sons¦191110 were¦191112 Suah¦191112, Harnefer¦191113, Shual¦191114, Beri¦191115, Yimrah¦191116,
\v 37 Betser¦191118, Hod¦191119, Shama¦191120, Shilshah¦191121, Yitran¦191122 (\add whose other name was Yeter\add*), and¦191123 Be'era¦191123.
\p
\v 38 Yeter's¦191126 sons¦191125 were¦191127 Yefuneh¦191127, Pispah, and Ara¦191129.
\v 39 \add Another of Asher's descendants was\add* Ula¦191132, whose sons¦191131 were¦191133 Arah¦191133, Haniyel¦191134, and¦191135 Ritsya¦191135.
\p
\v 40 All¦191137 those¦191139 were¦191140 Asher's¦191142 descendants¦191140, \add ≈clan leaders\add*, chosen¦191147, very \add ≈strong¦191148\add*, \add ≈leaders¦191150 of¦191150 leaders¦191151\add*. And there were¦191156 26,000 men¦191156 listed in their¦191152 genealogy¦191152 in¦191153 the army¦191153.
\c 8
\s1 Benyamin's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Benjamin; The Genealogy of Saul the Benjamite; The Descendants of Benjamin
\p
\v 1 Benyamin¦191162 had sons: Bela¦191166, Ashbel¦191168, Ahrah¦191170,
\v 2 Nohah¦191173, and the¦191176 fifth¦191176, Rafa¦191175.
\p
\v 3 Bela's¦191181 sons¦191180 were¦191179 Adar¦191182, Gera¦191183, Avihud¦191184,
\v 4 Avishua¦191186, Na'aman¦191187, Ahoah¦191188,
\v 5 Gera¦191190, Shefufan, and Huram¦191192.
\p
\v 6 \add One of Gera's sons was\add* Ehud¦191196. \add ≈His descendants¦191195 were¦191199 leaders of their clans who lived¦191201\add* in Geva¦191202 \add city\add*, but¦191203 were taken¦191203 into¦191203 exile¦191203 to¦191204 Manahat¦191206.
\v 7 Ehud's sons were Na'aman¦191208, Ahiyah¦191209, and¦191210 Gera¦191210. \add @Gera\add* \add ≈was the one who led¦191212 them¦191212\add* \add when they moved to Manahat\add*. He¦191213 \add ≈was the father¦191213 of\add* Uza¦191216 and¦191217 Ahihud¦191219.
\p
\v 8 \add Another of Benyamin's\add* \add descendants was\add* Shaharayim, \add ≈who lived\add* in¦191223 the¦191223 Moav¦191224 \add !(Moab¦191224)\add* countryside¦191223. He¦191227 \add ≈divorced\add* his¦191233 wives¦191233 Hushim¦191229 and¦191230 Ba'ara¦191232,
\v 9 and¦191235 fathered¦191235 \add sons\add* with his¦191239 wife¦191239 Hodesh¦191238: Yovav¦191242, Tsivya¦191245, Meysha¦191248, Malkam¦191251,
\v 10 Ye'uts¦191255, Shaveyah, and Mirmah¦191261. They all became leaders of their clans.
\v 11 (He'd¦191268 fathered¦191268 Avituv¦191271 and¦191272 Elpa'al¦191274 by Hushim¦191267.)
\p
\v 12 Elpa'al's¦191277 sons¦191276 were¦191278 Ever¦191278, Misham¦191279, Shemer¦191280 (who built¦191282 Ono¦191285 \add city\add*, plus¦191286 Lod¦191288 and¦191289 its¦191289 towns),
\s2 Benyamin's descendants in Gat and Ayalon
\rem /s1 The Benjaminites in Gath and Aijalon
\p
\v 13 plus¦191291 Beriah¦191291 and¦191292 Shema¦191292, who were¦191294 leaders of their clans, who lived¦191296 in Ayalon¦191297 \add city\add*. They forced the¦191302 people who lived in Gat¦191303 \add city\add* to flee.
\v 14-16 Beriah's¦191317 sons¦191316 were Ahyo¦191305, Shashak¦191306, Yerimot¦191307, Zevadyah¦191309, Arad¦191310, Eder¦191311, Mika'el¦191313 \add !(Michael¦191313)\add*, Yishpah¦191314, and¦191315 Yoha¦191315.
\s2 Benyamin's descendants in Yerushalem
\rem /s1 The Benjaminites in Jerusalem
\p
\v 17-18 Elpa'al's¦191328 \add other\add* descendants¦191327 included Zevadyah¦191319, Meshulam¦191320, Hizki¦191321, Hever¦191322, Yishmeray¦191324, Yizliah¦191325, and¦191326 Yovav¦191326.
\p
\v 19-21 \add Another of Benyamin's\add* \add descendants was Shimei.\add* Shimei's¦191342 descendants¦191341 \add ≈included\add* Yakim¦191330, Zikri¦191331, Zavdi¦191332, Eli'eynay¦191334, Tsiletay¦191335, Eliel¦191336, Adayah¦191338, Berayah¦191339, and¦191340 Shimrat¦191340.
\p
\v 22-25 Shashak's¦191360 sons¦191359 were Yishpan¦191344, Ever¦191345, Eliel¦191346, Avdon¦191348, Zikri¦191349, Hanan¦191350, Hananyah¦191352, Eylam¦191353, Antotiyah¦191354, Yifdeyah¦191356, and¦191357 Penuel¦191357.
\p
\v 26-27 \add Another of Benyamin's descendants was\add* Yeroham¦191370, whose sons¦191369 were Shamsheray¦191362, Sheharyah, Atalyah¦191364 \add !(Athaliah¦191364)\add*, Ya'areshyah¦191366, Eliyah¦191367 \add !(Elijah¦191367)\add*, and¦191368 Zikri¦191368.
\p
\v 28 \add @All those descendants of Elpa'al (Shaharayim's son)\add* were¦191373 \add ≈leaders¦191376 of¦191373 their clans according to¦191375 their¦191375 genealogies\add*. They¦191378 \add all\add* lived¦191378 in¦191379 Yerushalem¦191379 \add !(Jerusalem¦191379)\add*.
\s2 Benyamin's descendants in Giveon
\rem /s1 The Family of Saul; The Benjaminites in Gibeon and Jerusalem
\p
\v 29 \add Another of Benyamin's\add* \add descendants was Yeiel\add*—he \add ≈was a founder¦191384 of¦191384\add* Giveon¦191385 \add city\add*. His¦191387 wife¦191387 was¦191388 Ma'akah¦191388.
\v 30 His¦191390 sons¦191390, \add starting with\add* the¦191391 eldest¦191391, were¦191392 Avdon¦191392, Tsur¦191393, Kish¦191394, Ba'al¦191395, Nadav¦191396,
\v 31 Gedor¦191398, Ahyo¦191399, and¦191400 Zeker¦191400.
\v 32 Miklot¦191402 \add (another of Yeiel's sons)\add* was Shimeah's¦191406 father¦191403. All those descendants of Yeiel also¦191407 lived¦191412 in¦191413 Yerushalem¦191413 \add !(Jerusalem¦191413)\add* near their¦191416 relatives¦191411.
\s1 The descendants of King Sha'ul (Saul)
\rem /s1 The Family of King Saul
\p
\v 33 Ner¦191419 was the father¦191420 of Kish¦191423. Kish¦191424 was the father¦191425 of \add King\add* Sha'ul¦191428 \add !(Saul¦191428)\add*. Sha'ul¦191429 was the father¦191430 of Yonatan¦191433 \add !(Jonathan¦191433)\add*, Malkishua¦191438, Avinadav¦191441, and¦191442 Eshba'al¦191444.
\v 34 Yonatan's¦191448 son¦191446 was Meriv-Ba'al. Meriv-Ba'al was the father¦191453 of Mikah¦191456.
\p
\v 35 Mikah's¦191460 sons¦191459 were¦191461 Piton¦191461, Melek¦191462, Ta'area¦191463, and¦191464 Ahaz¦191464.
\v 36 Ahaz¦191466 was the father¦191467 of Yeho'adah¦191470. Yeho'adah¦191471 was the father¦191472 of Alemet¦191475, Azmavet¦191478, and¦191479 Zimri¦191481. Zimri¦191482 was the father¦191483 of Motsa¦191486.
\v 37 Motsa¦191488 was the father¦191489 of Binea¦191492. Rafa¦191493 was¦191494 \add @Binea's¦191494\add* son. Eleasah¦191495 was Rafa's son. Atsel¦191497 was \add @Eleasah's¦191498\add* son¦191498.
\p
\v 38 Atsel¦191500 had \add ≈a total¦191512 of\add* six¦191501 sons¦191502, named¦191504 Azrikam¦191505, Bokru¦191507, Yishmael¦191508 \add !(Ishmael¦191508)\add*, Shearyah¦191509, Ovadyah¦191510 \add !(Obadiah¦191510)\add*, and¦191511 Hanan¦191511.
\v 39 \add @Atsel¦191520\add* had a brother¦191520, Eshek. Eshek's oldest son¦191518 was¦191521 Ulam¦191521. His¦191522 other \add ≡sons\add* were¦191523 Ye'ush¦191523 and¦191525 Elifelet¦191525.
\p
\v 40 Ulam's¦191531 sons¦191529 were¦191528 powerful warriors who¦191536 were also¦191538 archers. Altogether they had¦191538 150 sons¦191540 and¦191543 grandsons.
\p All¦191544 those¦191546 were Benyamin's¦191548 descendants.
\c 9
\s1 Those who returned from captivity
\rem /s1 The restored community; The People Who Returned from Captivity
